const text: LessonText[][] = [[{"say":"IB History is about evidence and argument, not just memorizing facts.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Paper 1 tests source skills: comprehension, comparing and contrasting sources, and evaluating origin, purpose, content (OPCVL: origin, purpose, content, value, limitation). A source's value and limitations depend on who made it, when, why and for whom."},{"t":"p","text":"Historians build arguments around concepts: causation, consequence, continuity, change, significance and perspective. Historiography studies how interpretations differ (for example, orthodox, revisionist and post-revisionist views of the Cold War)."},{"t":"p","text":"Essays need a clear thesis, specific evidence (names, dates, statistics), analysis rather than narrative, and a balanced evaluation reaching a reasoned conclusion."}]},{"say":"The prescribed subject compares struggles for rights in the US and South Africa.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"US civil rights movement (1954–1965): Brown v. Board of Education (1954), the Montgomery bus boycott (1955–56), Little Rock (1957), sit-ins and Freedom Rides (1960–61), the Birmingham campaign and March on Washington (1963), the Civil Rights Act (1964) and the Voting Rights Act (1965). Key figures: Martin Luther King Jr., Rosa Parks, Malcolm X; organizations: NAACP, SCLC, SNCC."},{"t":"p","text":"Apartheid South Africa (1948–1964): the National Party introduced apartheid laws (Population Registration Act, Group Areas Act, pass laws). Resistance: the ANC's Defiance Campaign (1952), the Freedom Charter (1955), the Sharpeville massacre (1960), the shift to armed struggle (Umkhonto we Sizwe) and the Rivonia Trial (1963–64), which imprisoned Nelson Mandela."}]}],[{"say":"Why do wars start, how are they fought, and what changes because of them?","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Causes are long-term (nationalism, imperial rivalry, alliances, ideology), short-term and immediate triggers. World War I: militarism, alliances, imperialism and nationalism (MAIN), triggered by the assassination of Franz Ferdinand (1914). World War II: the Treaty of Versailles, the Great Depression, fascist expansion and appeasement, triggered by the invasion of Poland (1939)."},{"t":"p","text":"Practices: total war mobilized economies and civilians; technology (tanks, aircraft, atomic bombs) changed warfare. Effects: territorial changes, the League of Nations and the United Nations, decolonization and the Cold War."},{"t":"p","text":"Wars can be compared across regions (for example, the Spanish Civil War or the Chinese Civil War)."}]},{"say":"The 20th century saw new kinds of dictatorship and a global rivalry between superpowers.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Authoritarian states (Hitler's Germany, Stalin's USSR, Mao's China, Castro's Cuba) emerged amid crisis. Leaders consolidated power through propaganda, terror, the cult of personality and control of the economy, education and religion."},{"t":"p","text":"The Cold War (1945–1991): US capitalism versus Soviet communism. Containment (Truman Doctrine, Marshall Plan), the Berlin Blockade (1948–49), NATO and the Warsaw Pact, the Korean War, the Cuban Missile Crisis (1962), Vietnam, détente (SALT) and the collapse of the USSR (1991)."},{"t":"p","text":"Historians debate responsibility: orthodox views blame Soviet expansion, revisionists blame US economic expansion, and post-revisionists share blame."}]}]];
export default text;
